INT. NATURAL HISTORY TAXIDERMY ANNEX - AFTER HOURS

A long shelved room of glass eyes and stuffed things. A moose head presides over a card table. Dust floats in the beam of a desk lamp.

LOUISA TRAN-WOLCOTT, fifties, in cardigan and reading glasses, sorts pencils at a desk. She is the night curator, and she is very bored.

A figure in a delivery uniform enters through the loading door with a hand truck and a crate. It is a MAN, thirties, with a clipboard.

DELIVERY MAN
Package for Mr. Abernathy.

LOUISA
Abernathy retired in March.

DELIVERY MAN
Then I'm in the wrong place.

LOUISA
Looks like it.

He doesn't leave. He pulls the crate toward the moose. Louisa watches him over her glasses.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
Is that a bird?

DELIVERY MAN
It's an owl.

LOUISA
Oh. Which?

DELIVERY MAN
Barn. Dead.

LOUISA
They're all dead in here, dear.

He smiles, finally. Sets the clipboard down. A SECOND MAN, bulky, steps through the loading door, unnoticed by Louisa, and begins loosening the screws on a display case.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
(to the Delivery Man)
You'll have to sign in. There's a ledger.

DELIVERY MAN
Sure. Name?

LOUISA
Mine?

DELIVERY MAN
Yours.

LOUISA
Louisa.

DELIVERY MAN
Louisa. I'm Pike.

PIKE
(to the Second Man)
She's Louisa.

SECOND MAN
Thanks, Pike.

Louisa turns. The Second Man holds up a stuffed fox in one hand and a velvet bag in the other.

LOUISA
That's the Kensington fox.

SECOND MAN
Is it?

LOUISA
It's worth nothing.

SECOND MAN
Then you won't mind.

LOUISA
Please don't.

PIKE ^
Please don't.

The Second Man pauses. The fox's glass eye catches the lamp.

SECOND MAN
(to Pike)
You said she'd be asleep.

PIKE
She was supposed to be.

LOUISA
I never sleep. I'm a curator.

She picks up her pencils, one by one, and sets them in a mug.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
Take the fox. Leave the moose. He's been here longer than I have.

The Second Man nods. Pike nods. They leave with the fox and the owl crate, and the moose watches them go.
